WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.740
"Wednesday" has been the talk of TV&nbsp;
Twitter lately, but not always for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.640
a good reason. So what's really going&nbsp;
on with the sudden Tim Burton backlash?

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.620
After debuting on November 23, the Netflix&nbsp;
series managed to rack up positive reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.200
and record-breaking streaming numbers,&nbsp;
with more than 341 million hours watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.120
in its first week on the app, according to&nbsp;
Netflix. No other English-language TV show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.260
has been able to bring in those results for&nbsp;
the streamer over the course of its first week.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.160
The supernatural coming-of-age&nbsp;
story follows Jenna Ortega's.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.380
Wednesday Addams as she adjusts to life at&nbsp;
Nevermore Academy, a Vermont-based reform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.580
school for other peculiar outcasts like&nbsp;
herself in the fictional town of Jericho.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.920
On the surface, "Wednesday" is a show that seems&nbsp;
innocent and fun, with a familiar premise based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.180
on "The Addams Family" characters and franchise.&nbsp;
However, there's something that many fans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.500
found extremely wrong with the spinoff, which&nbsp;
is getting talked about a lot as of late.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.420
For some "Wednesday" viewers, it feels like the&nbsp;
creative team, including executive producer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.120
director Tim Burton, chose to cast Black actors&nbsp;
as the show's main antagonists. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.720
fans have been taking to social&nbsp;
media to voice their outrage.

00:01:09.893 --> 00:01:10.931
One user tweeted,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.260
The casting choices that viewers are&nbsp;
most upset about are New York-born actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.820
Joy Sunday — who plays resident mean&nbsp;
girl Bianca Barclay at Nevermore — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.200
Hollywood newcomer Iman Marson, who&nbsp;
plays Lucas Walker, a local bully.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.480
Walker's family runs the show's fictional&nbsp;
Pilgrim World. His father, Noble Walker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.200
is the mayor of Jericho and owns&nbsp;
the colonizer-themed theme park.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.380
"It takes a special kind of stupid to devote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.514
an entire theme park to zealots&nbsp;
responsible for mass genocide."

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.960
It's easy to see how casting Black actors to&nbsp;
run such a place could be considered tone-deaf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.600
which is exactly what people&nbsp;
are calling it on social media.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.980
Self-described "Anti-Racism&nbsp;
Consultant" Jon Cornejo tweeted:

00:02:24.752 --> 00:02:30.420
A lack of diversity in Tim Burton's projects&nbsp;
has been an issue over the years. After making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:36.060
the 2016 fantasy flick "Miss Peregrine's&nbsp;
Home for Peculiar Children," Burton was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.980
called out for having a predominately white&nbsp;
cast, with Samuel L. Jackson being one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.320
the only non-white stars — and serving as an&nbsp;
antagonist. Burton told Bustle at the time:

